post_install() {
  cat <<'EOF'
Honcho needs PostgreSQL (with the pgvector, pg_trgm and btree_gin
extensions) and an LLM provider key to do useful work:

  1. Create the database role and database, then enable the extensions:
       sudo -u postgres createuser --pwprompt honcho
       sudo -u postgres createdb -O honcho honcho
       sudo -u postgres psql -d honcho -c 'CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS vector;'
       sudo -u postgres psql -d honcho -c 'CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS pg_trgm;'
       sudo -u postgres psql -d honcho -c 'CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS btree_gin;'

  2. Configure /etc/honcho/honcho.env (DB_CONNECTION_URI plus at least one
     LLM_*_API_KEY; without a key the API still starts, but memory derivation
     and embeddings fail). An OpenAI-compatible proxy (OpenRouter, Ollama,
     vLLM...) also works; see /usr/share/doc/honcho/env.template.

  3. Start the API and the background deriver worker:
       sudo systemctl enable --now honcho honcho-deriver

Both units run as the unprivileged "honcho" system user created by this
package. Database migrations run automatically at API startup
(advisory-locked). The API listens on 127.0.0.1:8000 - put a reverse proxy
(Caddy, nginx) in front of it for TLS.
Docs: https://honcho.dev/docs/v3/contributing/self-hosting
EOF
}

post_upgrade() {
  echo "Restart the services to apply migrations:"
  echo "  sudo systemctl restart honcho honcho-deriver"
}
